(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["coronary"]="<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-separate-blood-supply\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> Why does the heart need a separate blood supply?</h2>\n<p>The blood inside the chambers can't nourish the thick ventricular walls: oxygen can only diffuse a fraction of a millimetre. So the heart has its own arteries, the <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong>. They arise from the <strong><span data-term=\"aortic-sinuses\">aortic sinuses</span></strong>, the small pouches just above the aortic valve cusps.</p>\n<p><strong>The heart already takes out most of its oxygen at rest</strong> (about 70%, compared with about 25% for the body as a whole). So when it works harder, it can't simply extract more. <strong>Coronary blood flow has to rise.</strong> <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Oxygen delivered = coronary blood flow × oxygen content of the blood.</strong> If either falls, the heart muscle runs short.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-trace-coronary-inflow-and-venous-drainage\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Trace coronary inflow and venous drainage</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Anatomical anterior view of the human heart showing the left and right ventricles and coronary arteries running over their surfaces\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Anterior heart and ventricular coronary vessels.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0260_CoronaryVessels_Anterior.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_arteries.svg?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Medical illustration of the heart with labelled right coronary, left coronary, left anterior descending, circumflex and posterior descending coronary artery branches.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Coronary arteries in their anatomical locations.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">Patrick J. Lynch; derivative and labels by Fred the Oyster and Mikael Häggström, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_arteries.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Left main coronary artery</strong> → divides into:<ul>\n<li>the <strong>left anterior descending (LAD)</strong>, running down the front, which supplies the <strong>front of the left ventricle and most of the septum</strong>;</li>\n<li>the <strong>left circumflex (LCx)</strong>, curving round the left side, which supplies the <strong>side wall of the left ventricle</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Right coronary artery (RCA)</strong> supplies the <strong>right ventricle</strong> and, usually, the <strong>bottom (inferior) wall</strong> of the heart.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"coronary-dominance\">Dominance</span></strong> just means which artery gives off the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, which supplies the back of the septum. It is the RCA in most people (&quot;right dominant&quot;). It does <strong>not</strong> mean that artery supplies one whole side.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 1. Major coronary routes and drainage — simplified flow map</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 680 250\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Aortic root branches into left main and right coronary arteries. Left main divides into LAD and circumflex; posterior descending artery origin defines dominance. Venous return passes chiefly via coronary sinus to right atrium\"><g font-family=\"Arial,sans-serif\" fill=\"#22354e\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><rect x=\"240\" y=\"9\" width=\"200\" height=\"38\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e8f0ff\" stroke=\"#638dce\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"34\" font-size=\"15\">Aortic root</text><rect x=\"67\" y=\"84\" width=\"210\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"172\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Left main coronary</text><rect x=\"405\" y=\"84\" width=\"212\" height=\"43\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e6f3ff\" stroke=\"#6d96c7\"/><text x=\"511\" y=\"111\" font-size=\"15\">Right coronary</text><rect x=\"16\" y=\"160\" width=\"152\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"92\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">LAD</text><text x=\"92\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">anterior + septal</text><rect x=\"183\" y=\"160\" width=\"150\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#e8f6ec\" stroke=\"#70a987\"/><text x=\"258\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">Circumflex</text><text x=\"258\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"12\">lateral LV</text><rect x=\"404\" y=\"160\" width=\"250\" height=\"48\" rx=\"9\" fill=\"#fff0da\" stroke=\"#c4a16b\"/><text x=\"529\" y=\"178\" font-size=\"14\">PDA origin defines</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"13\">coronary dominance</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"239\" font-size=\"13\">Venous blood: cardiac veins → coronary sinus → right atrium</text></g><g stroke=\"#5278a5\" stroke-width=\"2.6\" fill=\"none\"><path d=\"M340 47v18H172v19M340 65h171v19M172 127v16H92v17M172 143h86v17M511 127v33M333 183h71\"/></g></svg><p>Conceptual branches, not an anatomical map of coronary vessel positions. Dominance and individual territories vary.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>Drainage:</strong> most coronary venous blood collects in the <strong><span data-term=\"coronary-sinus\">coronary sinus</span></strong>, which empties into the <strong>right atrium</strong>. A little drains straight into the chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-left-ventricular-coronary-perfusion-greatest-during-diastole\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> Why is left ventricular coronary perfusion greatest during diastole?</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Left coronary blood flow through one cardiac cycle — qualitative waveform</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 650 235\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Illustrative left coronary flow falls during early systolic contraction, remains relatively low in systole and rises sharply in early diastole before gradually declining\"><g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#667b93\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M62 24v172h560\"/></g><rect x=\"88\" y=\"28\" width=\"240\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#fff0e6\"/><rect x=\"328\" y=\"28\" width=\"275\" height=\"164\" fill=\"#e9f5ff\"/><path d=\"M88 88C112 93 117 159 150 162S255 133 328 143C347 145 351 32 380 39C432 38 472 75 509 94S571 109 603 121\" stroke=\"#1d72c8\" stroke-width=\"5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"#293d58\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"140\" y=\"47\">Systole</text><text x=\"427\" y=\"47\">Diastole</text><text x=\"13\" y=\"19\">Flow</text><text x=\"257\" y=\"220\">Time →</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"211\">S1</text><text x=\"315\" y=\"211\">S2</text></g></svg><p>Conceptual shape, not measured patient data: left-ventricular compression restricts flow during systole; relaxation allows a marked early-diastolic increase. Right coronary flow may be less strongly diastolic.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>In systole, the left ventricle squeezes its own arteries shut.</strong> The coronary branches run through the muscle wall. When the muscle contracts hard, it <strong>compresses</strong> them, and left ventricular coronary flow almost stops.</p>\n<p><strong>In diastole, the muscle relaxes and the arteries open.</strong> Aortic pressure is still high, so blood <strong>rushes in</strong>.</p>\n<p>So <strong>the left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole.</strong> The right ventricle, which squeezes far less hard, is fed in both systole and diastole.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 2. Myocardial compression explains diastolic left-coronary flow</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"During systole intramyocardial vessels are compressed; during diastole their compression is relieved and left ventricular coronary flow increases\"><rect x=\"12\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fce9ec\" stroke=\"#bf6777\"/><rect x=\"338\" y=\"20\" width=\"270\" height=\"112\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f3ff\" stroke=\"#668dbf\"/><g font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"50\">Systole</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"50\">Diastole</text></g><g font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#24364d\"><text x=\"147\" y=\"77\">Muscle squeezes vessels</text><text x=\"147\" y=\"102\">LV flow is restricted</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"77\">Muscle relaxes</text><text x=\"473\" y=\"102\">LV flow increases</text></g><path d=\"M289 78h40m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#456d95\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Qualitative comparison; the amount of systolic flow depends on vessel depth, ventricular pressures and the territory being supplied.</p></figure>\n\n<p><strong>The pressure that drives left coronary flow</strong> ≈ <strong>aortic diastolic pressure − left ventricular diastolic pressure.</strong> Anything that lowers aortic diastolic pressure (shock, aortic regurgitation) or raises left ventricular filling pressure (heart failure) cuts this driving pressure.</p>\n<h3>Why is the inner layer (subendocardium) most at risk?</h3>\n<p>The <strong>inner layer</strong> of the left ventricle is squeezed hardest, because it is closest to the high-pressure cavity. So it depends <strong>most</strong> on diastole, and it is the <strong>first to become ischaemic</strong> when perfusion falls. In <strong>aortic stenosis</strong>, for example, very high ventricular pressure both raises oxygen demand and squeezes the inner-layer vessels harder.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–3\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–3 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–3</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Why must coronary flow rise when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The heart already extracts most of the oxygen from its blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The coronary arteries narrow during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Blood carries more oxygen during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The heart can store extra oxygen for later use</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> With about 70% extraction at rest, there is little more to take, so more oxygen needs more flow.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does the left ventricle receive most of its coronary flow in diastole?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Aortic pressure is highest in diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Its contracting muscle squeezes its own arteries in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The coronary arteries close their valves in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The right ventricle takes all the flow during systole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Contraction compresses the arteries running through the wall. When the muscle relaxes, they open and fill.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Which part of the left ventricle becomes ischaemic first when perfusion falls?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. The outer layer, nearest the surface arteries</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The inner layer (subendocardium), squeezed hardest in systole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The papillary muscles only</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. All layers equally at the same time</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The inner layer is compressed most and depends most on diastolic flow.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-coronary-flow-match-changing-myocardial-work\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> How does coronary flow match changing myocardial work?</h2>\n<p>The heart's oxygen demand rises with <strong>heart rate</strong>, <strong>contractility</strong> and <strong>wall tension</strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/co-measurement?section=7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-heart-s-oxygen-demand\">→ Measuring Cardiac Output, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Working heart muscle opens its own arteries.</strong> As it works harder, it releases local signals, chiefly <strong><span data-term=\"adenosine\">adenosine</span></strong> and also K⁺, low O₂ and <strong>nitric oxide</strong> from the endothelium. These relax the coronary arterioles, so flow rises to match the work. This is <strong><span data-term=\"active-hyperaemia\">active hyperaemia</span></strong>. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/local-regulation\">→ Local Regulation</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic nerves: two effects, one winner.</strong> They can constrict coronary arteries directly (α receptors). But they also make the heart beat faster and harder (β₁), which raises its metabolism and releases dilating metabolites. <strong>The dilation wins</strong>, so in exercise coronary flow rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-tachycardia-cause-ischaemia\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> Why can tachycardia cause ischaemia?</h2>\n<p>A fast heart hits the heart muscle from <strong>both</strong> sides:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Demand rises:</strong> more beats per minute means more work.</li>\n<li><strong>Supply time falls:</strong> as heart rate rises, <strong>diastole shortens much more than systole</strong>. Diastole is when the left ventricle is perfused.</li>\n</ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"diastole-clock\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Diastole clock</figcaption><p>A heart-rate slider (60–180 beats/min). One beat is drawn as a bar split into systole and diastole, to scale. Below it: one minute's worth of diastole, in seconds. Readout: \"At 70: 0.86 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.57 s. At 150: 0.40 s per beat, diastole ≈ 0.16 s.\" A line warns when diastole falls below about a third of the beat: \"the left ventricle has little time to be perfused\".</p></figure>\n\n<p>In a healthy heart, the arterioles dilate enough to cope. But with a <strong>narrowed coronary artery</strong>, a <strong>low diastolic pressure</strong> or a <strong>high filling pressure</strong>, the shrinking diastole can tip the balance into <strong>ischaemia</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-coronary-flow-reserve-angina-and-myocardial-infarction\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> Coronary flow reserve, angina and myocardial infarction</h2>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration regional-anatomy-figure\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=800\" srcset=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=480 480w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=800 800w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Coronary_Artery_Disease.png?width=1200 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 700px) 92vw, 760px\" alt=\"Medical comparison of normal coronary artery flow and an artery narrowed by atherosclerotic plaque.\"><figcaption><strong>Figure. Normal coronary artery versus a narrowed vessel.</strong><details class=\"figure-attribution\"><summary>Image credit</summary><span class=\"figure-credit\">BruceBlaus, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronary_Artery_Disease.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></details></figcaption></figure>\n\n<p><strong><span data-term=\"flow-reserve\">Coronary flow reserve</span></strong> is how much coronary flow can rise above its resting level when the arterioles fully dilate. Normally, it can rise about 4–5 times.</p>\n<p><strong>Why <span data-term=\"angina\">angina</span> comes on with exertion, not at rest:</strong> behind a narrowed artery, the arterioles <strong>dilate at rest</strong> to keep flow normal. They have <strong>used up their reserve</strong>. When exercise raises demand, there is nothing left to give, so supply falls short and <strong>angina</strong> (ischaemic chest pain) appears. Resting cures it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Angina</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Myocardial infarction (heart attack)</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>What happens</td>\n  <td>Temporary <strong>ischaemia</strong>: supply falls short of demand</td>\n  <td>Prolonged, severe ischaemia: <strong>heart muscle dies</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Reversible?</td>\n  <td><strong>Yes</strong>, when demand falls or flow returns</td>\n  <td><strong>No</strong>: dead muscle becomes scar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Usual cause</td>\n  <td>A fixed narrowing plus higher demand</td>\n  <td>A plaque ruptures and a clot blocks the artery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 4–6\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 4–6 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 4–6</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> What mainly dilates the coronary arterioles when the heart works harder?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Parasympathetic nerves releasing acetylcholine</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Local signals such as adenosine from working heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Direct sympathetic stimulation of α receptors</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. A rise in coronary venous pressure</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Metabolic signals from the working muscle (adenosine, K⁺, low O₂, nitric oxide) relax the arterioles: active hyperaemia.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why can a heart rate of 160 cause ischaemia in a patient with coronary narrowing?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It raises oxygen demand and shortens diastole, when the left ventricle is fed</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises aortic diastolic pressure too high</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It makes the coronary arteries contract more strongly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It increases the time the left ventricle is perfused</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Demand rises while the time available for left ventricular perfusion shrinks.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A man has chest pain on climbing stairs that goes when he rests. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A coronary artery is completely blocked, killing heart muscle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A narrowed artery has used up its reserve, so supply can't rise with demand</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. His heart muscle is extracting too little oxygen</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. His coronary veins are blocked during exercise</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> The arterioles behind the narrowing are already dilated at rest. On exertion, supply can't rise, which gives stable angina.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"7-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Coronary arteries</strong> arise from the aortic sinuses. The heart extracts about 70% of its oxygen at rest, so more demand needs <strong>more flow</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>LAD</strong> = front of the LV and the septum; <strong>circumflex</strong> = side of the LV; <strong>RCA</strong> = right ventricle and inferior wall. <strong>Dominance</strong> = which artery gives the posterior descending artery.</li>\n<li><strong>Venous blood</strong> drains via the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium.</li>\n<li><strong>The left ventricle is fed mainly in diastole</strong>, because contraction squeezes its arteries. Driving pressure ≈ aortic diastolic − LV diastolic pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>The <span data-term=\"subendocardium\">subendocardium</span></strong> is the most vulnerable layer.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia:</strong> adenosine and other metabolites dilate the arterioles. Sympathetic β₁ effects make the net result dilation.</li>\n<li><strong>Tachycardia</strong> raises demand and <strong>shortens diastole</strong>, so it can cause ischaemia in diseased arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Narrowed artery</strong> → reserve used up at rest → <strong>angina on exertion</strong>. <strong>Infarction</strong> = muscle death from prolonged ischaemia.</li>\n</ol>\n";
